Hmm, four args. Alternatively define a `UnitOfWork`/transaction helper. Let me just add `coupons: CouponRepository` and `db: Db`.

Actually eligibility check + writes: transaction. Implementation:

```ts
applyCoupon(id, couponCode): Order {
  const order = this.get(id);
  if (order.status !== "PENDING") throw conflict("ORDER_NOT_PENDING", ...);
  if (order.couponCode !== null) throw conflict("ORDER_ALREADY_HAS_COUPON", ...);
  if (typeof couponCode !== "string" || couponCode.trim() === "") throw badRequest("INVALID_REQUEST", ...);
  const coupon = this.coupons.findByCode(couponCode);
  if (!coupon) throw notFound("COUPON_NOT_FOUND", ...);
  if (coupon.status !== "ACTIVE") throw conflict("COUPON_NOT_ACTIVE", ...);
  const now = this.clock().toISOString();
  if (now < coupon.validFrom) throw conflict("COUPON_NOT_YET_VALID", ...);
  if (now > coupon.validUntil) throw conflict("COUPON_EXPIRED", ...);
  if (coupon.usageLimit !== null && coupon.usedCount >= coupon.usageLimit) throw conflict("COUPON_USAGE_LIMIT_REACHED", ...);
  if (order.amountCents < coupon.minAmountCents) throw conflict("ORDER_BELOW_MIN_AMOUNT", ...);
  const discount = this.computeDiscount(coupon, order.amountCents);
  const final = order.amountCents - discount;
  const at = this.clock().toISOString();
  // transaction
  this.orders.applyCoupon(id, coupon.code, discount, final);
  this.orders.recordRedemption(id, coupon.code, discount, at);
  this.coupons.incrementUsedCount(coupon.code);
  return this.get(id);
}
```

Wait now taken twice; use one `now` for both checks and redemption timestamp.

ISO string comparison: validFrom could be "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z" while now.toISOString() is "2026-10-01T08:00:00.000Z". Lexical compare works mostly since both start with YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS. "2026-10-01T08:00:00.000Z" vs "2027-01-01T00:00:00Z" - compare char by char: at position of seconds "00.000Z" vs "00Z": '.' (0x2E) < 'Z' (0x5A), so "2026-...00.000Z" < "...00Z" fine for same-second boundary edge. Minor. Better compare Date.parse values. Coupon validFrom parse to ms and compare with now.getTime(). More robust. Let me do that.

Also validUntil inclusive: now > validUntil means expired; if equal, valid. Use Date.parse.

Now transactions: define in db.ts a helper? Or just use db.exec. node:sqlite DatabaseSync has .exec. I'll add to OrderService a db. Actually cleaner: add a `withTransaction` method to repositories? Hmm, transaction spans multiple repos. Let me add a small helper in db.ts:

```ts
export function withTransaction<T>(db: Db, fn: () => T): T {
  db.exec("BEGIN");
  try { const r = fn(); db.exec("COMMIT"); return r; }
  catch (e) { db.exec("ROLLBACK"); throw e; }
}
```

Then OrderService takes db. Fine.

Alternatively, to avoid injecting Db into service (which breaks layering somewhat — services shouldn't touch db directly), I could put both order writes in OrderRepository and redemption in... they're different tables/repos. Hmm. The pattern "all SQL lives in repositories" — withTransaction is SQL-ish but a generic helper. Acceptable. Repos each hold db anyway. I'll pass db and use withTransaction helper. Services orchestrating a transaction is normal.

